course: physics
lesson: 27
title: Batteries, circuits and how electricity flows
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Electricity only flows when it has a complete loop to go round, from the battery, through the bulb and back again; that loop is called a circuit.
explain: Push the switch on a torch and you close a gap in the loop, so the bulb lights; push it again and the gap opens, and the light goes out at once.
explain: A battery does not hold a tankful of electricity: it holds chemicals that push electricity round the loop, and when those chemicals are used up the battery is flat.
explain: Metals such as copper let electricity through easily, while plastic, rubber and wood block it, which is why every wire wears a plastic coat.
explain: When electricity squeezes through a thin wire it heats it up; that is how an old bulb glows, how a toaster browns bread, and why a charger feels warm.
fact: Electricity flows only round a complete loop called a circuit; a switch opens or closes a gap in that loop.
fact: A battery stores energy in chemicals and pushes electricity round the circuit until the chemicals are used up; a charger can push the chemicals of a rechargeable battery back.
fact: Two batteries in a row push about twice as hard as one, so more electricity flows; put in the wrong way round, they push against each other.
fact: Metals let electricity flow because they have loose electrons that can drift along; plastic, rubber and wood block it.
fact: The plastic coat on a wire stops the electricity escaping into whatever the wire touches.
fact: A thin wire resists the flow and gets hot: an old bulb's wire glows white hot, and a toaster's special wires glow orange on purpose.
fact: Modern LED bulbs waste much less energy as heat than old bulbs with a glowing wire.
fact: A fuse has a thin wire that melts if too much electricity flows, breaking the circuit before the wires in the walls overheat; plugging too many things into one socket can make too much flow and overheat it.
fact: In some strings of Christmas lights all the bulbs are in one loop, so one broken bulb stops the flow to all of them; newer strings give each bulb its own path.
fact: A power station turns big machines that push electricity along wires on tall pylons to our homes, at very high voltage.
fact: No machine passes on all the electricity it gets; some always turns into heat, which is why a charger warms up.
exercise: What does a switch on a torch really do? | It closes or opens a gap in the loop of the circuit | It fills the bulb up with fresh new electricity each time | It squeezes the battery to make it work | It turns the bulb round to face the front
exercise: Why does a battery run out after a while? | The chemicals that push the electricity get used up | The electricity slowly leaks out through its plastic case | The battery gets tired from being carried around all day | The bulb slowly sucks all of the metal out of the battery
exercise: What does a battery really store? | Energy in chemicals | A tankful of electricity | Tiny bits of light | Hot air under pressure
exercise: Why does a toy with two batteries in a row go faster than with one? | Two batteries push the electricity about twice as hard | The second battery is only there as a spare for when one runs out | Two batteries make the toy much lighter | Two batteries cool the motor down
exercise: What happens if one of two batteries goes in the wrong way round? | They push against each other and the toy does not work | The toy runs backwards at double speed, in the wrong direction | The toy runs exactly the same as before | The batteries charge each other up
exercise: Which of these lets electricity flow easily? | A copper wire | A plastic ruler | A rubber band | A wooden spoon
exercise: Why are electric wires covered in plastic? | Plastic blocks electricity and stops it escaping | Plastic makes the electricity flow faster | Plastic stores spare electricity for later | Plastic keeps the copper from rusting in the light
exercise: Why does the thin wire in an old bulb glow white hot? | Electricity squeezing through a thin wire heats it | A tiny flame burns away inside the glass all the time | The glass collects sunlight during the day for night | The battery sends a hot liquid up through the thin wire
exercise: Why do a toaster's wires glow orange? | Electricity flowing through them makes them very hot | They are painted with glowing orange paint | The bread is burning against them | Orange light is reflected from the kitchen lamp above
exercise: What is a fuse for? | It melts and breaks the circuit if too much electricity flows | It stores electricity in case of a power cut | It makes the electricity in the house stronger | It turns the lights on when it gets dark
exercise: In an old string of Christmas lights, why does one broken bulb make them all go dark? | The bulbs are in one loop, and a break stops the flow to all | The broken bulb sucks power out of the others | The other bulbs go dark out of sympathy | The broken glass short-circuits the battery hidden in the plug
exercise: Where does the electricity in your house come from? | A power station far away, through wires on pylons | A big battery buried in the ground under the house | The rain that falls on the roof and runs down the pipes | The socket in the wall makes it all by itself, for free
exercise: Why does a phone charger warm up while charging? | Some of the electricity always turns into heat | The phone is sending its heat back up the wire | The charger burns a small amount of fuel inside | The plug rubs against the socket
exercise: Why are old bulbs being replaced by LED bulbs? | LEDs waste much less energy as heat | LEDs never need any electricity at all | LEDs make the room warmer in winter | Old bulbs are too bright to look at
exercise: What do metals have that lets electricity flow through them? | Loose electrons that can drift along | Tiny holes full of water running through | A thin coat of plastic all along the inside | Little air bubbles that carry sparks along
test: my little bro says the battery's full of electricity like juice in a carton. right? | No, it holds chemicals that push electricity round the circuit | Yes, and it pours out when you open the switch | Yes, that's why batteries are heavy | No, batteries are full of tiny lightning bolts
test: Sam joins a bulb to a battery with only one wire. Why doesn't it light? | There is no complete loop for the electricity to flow round | One wire is too short to carry electricity | The bulb needs to warm up first | The battery only works with a switch
test: In a cartoon, a robot fixes a frayed cable by wrapping it in tin foil. What's wrong with that? | Foil is metal and carries electricity, so it does not insulate | Foil is too shiny to touch electricity | Nothing, tin foil is the very best way to cover up a broken wire | Foil makes the electricity colder and slower
test: What would happen if a house had no fuses or trip switches at all? | Overloaded wires could overheat and start a fire | The lights would get brighter and brighter every day | Nothing, fuses are only there as decoration anyway | The house would not be able to get any electricity at all
test: ugh mum's got like 6 things plugged into one adaptor, chill or nah? | Not chill, too many things on one socket can overheat and start a fire | Totally chill, sockets can take anything | Fine, as long as the lights are off | Fine, the adaptor makes extra electricity
test: Why must nobody ever poke a knife into a toaster, even to get stuck toast out? | The wires inside are live and can give a deadly shock | The knife will make the toast taste of metal | The knife will cool the hot wires down and make them break | The bread will catch fire from the knife
test: Lily wants to charge her phone under her pillow overnight. Good idea? | No, the heat cannot escape and it could overheat | Yes, the pillow keeps the battery warm and happy | Yes, phones charge faster in the dark | No, the pillow will drain the battery
test: Jake sees wires on pylons high above the field. Why are they so high up? | They carry very high voltage, so they are kept far from people | Electricity flows better near the clouds | Birds would eat the wires if they were lower | It makes the electricity arrive faster
test: Which of these would complete a circuit if used to bridge a gap? | A steel paper clip | A plastic straw | A rubber eraser | A wooden lolly stick
test: one bulb on the tree went pop and the whole bloody string's dead. why?? | In that string the bulbs share one loop, and the break stops the flow | The bang scared the other bulbs off | The broken bulb drank the rest of the electricity | The plug is angry and switched itself off
test: Why can a rechargeable battery be used again but an ordinary one cannot? | A charger can push its chemicals back to how they were | Rechargeable batteries are filled up with fresh electricity | Ordinary batteries are just made of cheaper plastic | Rechargeable batteries never use their chemicals at all
exam: Identify the condition necessary for an electric current to flow through a lamp connected to a battery. | The provision of a complete and unbroken circuit | The presence of a plastic covering upon every conductor | The placement of the lamp nearer to the battery | The connection of the lamp by a single wire alone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of a fuse? | It melts and interrupts the circuit when an excessive current flows | It stores electrical energy against a failure of supply | It increases the voltage delivered to household appliances | It converts alternating current into stored chemical energy
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the contents of a battery. | Energy is stored in chemical form and released as a current | Electricity is retained within it in liquid form | It contains a small quantity of compressed lightning | It contains no energy at all until it is connected to a switch
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which copper conductors are enclosed in a plastic sheath. | Plastic does not conduct, thereby preventing the escape of current | Plastic accelerates the passage of the current | Plastic stores surplus charge for subsequent use | Plastic renders the copper magnetic
